Healthcare Access Anxiety / Institutional Mistrust - Adjustment Disorder - V65.49 (Unsatisfactory relationship with medical care)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Clinical environment panic, Care postponement, Anticipatory dismissal

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-020
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of a severe, neglected localized infection accompanied by extreme physiological panic upon the arrival of the medical clinician.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a rapidly spreading, painful skin infection (suspected cellulitis) that the patient had deliberately postponed treating. The patient refused to attend an Emergency Department due to severe institutional mistrust, necessitating an urgent home visit to prevent the progression to systemic sepsis prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My leg is burning and red, but I couldn't bring myself to go to the hospital because they never listen to me anyway.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic history of intense physiological arousal and panic when entering clinical environments, leading to the deliberate care postponement of preventative and acute medical needs. One week ago, the patient developed a minor scrape on their lower leg which has progressively worsened into a spreading, hot, painful erythema. Due to a deep-seated belief that the healthcare system is inherently unsafe and a previous documented history of experiencing medical bias, the patient delayed seeking care. Upon the clinician's arrival, the patient exhibited extreme clinical environment panic, a defensive and guarded communication style, and meticulous over-preparation of their medical history to legitimize the physical complaint, anticipating dismissal.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired by localized pain and severe acute anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of preventable severe morbidity or mortality (e.g., sepsis) due to chronic avoidance of the medical system.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 | HR: 118 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, highly anxious, sitting rigidly in a chair, clutching a binder of past medical records.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Integumentary: Right lower leg exhibits a 15cm x 10cm area of bright erythema, marked warmth, induration, and exquisite tenderness to palpation, consistent with acute cellulitis. No fluctuance or purulent drainage. Neurological: Alert and oriented. The exam is complicated by profound clinical environment panic; the patient's heart rate was 118 upon clinician entry, reflecting physical tension resulting in artificially elevated vital signs during triage. Anticipatory dismissal is evident as the patient repeatedly states, "I know you probably think this is just a rash, but look at my notes."
Environmental Observations: A table is covered with printed articles on the infection and neatly organized past medical history files.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescribed medications. Using unprescribed, culturally specific topical poultices on the leg.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Cellulitis secondary to Healthcare Access Anxiety / Institutional Mistrust - Adjustment Disorder - V65.49 (Unsatisfactory relationship with medical care) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a high fever (38.2), bright red superficial erythema, marked local warmth, and a clear history of a preceding cutaneous injury (scrape).

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the unilateral nature, presence of systemic fever, marked induration, and severe deep tenderness rather than superficial pruritus.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary psychological diagnosis; the severe panic and autonomic arousal are explicitly situational and triggered only by interaction with the medical system (white coat syndrome).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, rapidly progressing localized bacterial infection (cellulitis with fever 38.2) combined with severe, tachycardia-inducing (HR 118) institutional panic. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment in the home was absolutely critical because the patient explicitly refused to attend an Emergency Department due to severe medical mistrust. Delaying assessment until routine hours or forcing an ED visit would have resulted in complete care refusal, leading to a high probability of systemic sepsis and potential mortality, firmly satisfying the necessity for an urgent, home-based medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Outlined the border of the erythema with a surgical marker to track progression. Prescribed oral cephalexin 500mg four times daily for 7 days. Recommended the usual GP assess for 'white coat syndrome' via ambulatory blood pressure monitoring and evaluate scores on the Group-Based Medical Mistrust Scale.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely manage the infection and rebuild trust, implement a "shared-control tracking protocol": the patient will take a photograph of the marked leg every 12 hours and compare the redness to the pen line; taking objective, self-directed measurements empowers the patient and reduces the anticipatory dismissal anxiety by relying on shared factual data. To reduce clinical environment panic in future, schedule a brief, 5-minute "meet and greet" appointment with the regular GP when completely healthy; establishing a low-stakes, non-clinical interaction builds a baseline of safety outside of an acute crisis.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a patient advocate or liaison service to accompany the patient to future clinical appointments.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or after-hours review within 24 hours to ensure the cellulitis is responding to oral antibiotics and has not spread beyond the marked borders.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the redness spreads rapidly past the marked lines, if the fever spikes above 39.0°C, if uncontrollable shaking/chills develop, or if confusion occurs.

